      ],
      "blind_answer": 4,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "Prolonged heating with HI reduces all the -OH groups and the carbonyl group of glucose. This leaves the unbranched C6 chain as n-hexane, which shows the six carbons are in a straight chain."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u19_t02_2",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        2
      ],
      "blind_answer": 2,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "Nitric acid oxidises both the -CHO group and the terminal -CH2OH group to -COOH, giving saccharic (glucaric) acid. Formation of this dicarboxylic acid shows a primary alcohol group is present. Bromine water oxidises only the -CHO group."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u19_t02_3",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        3
      ],
      "blind_answer": 3,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "An open-chain aldehyde should form a bisulphite adduct with NaHSO3. Glucose does not, which only the cyclic hemiacetal structure explains. The other three facts are consistent with the open-chain structure."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u19_t02_4",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        3
      ],
      "blind_answer": 3,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "Fructose is a reducing sugar: in alkaline Tollens' medium it isomerises through an enediol and has a free hemiketal, so A is true. R is false because ketoses such as fructose are also reducing sugars."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u19_t02_5",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        1
      ],
      "blind_answer": 1,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "Statement I is correct: alpha- and beta-D-glucose are anomers differing only at C1, and the NCERT melting points are 419 K and 423 K. Statement II is correct: fructose's C5 -OH attacks C2 to form a five-membered furanose ring, while glucose forms a six-membered pyranose ring."
